saveOutputs: false
cells:
  - kind: 1
    value: "# 11e · Step 4 – Which part of the knee is injured? (RDF knowledge graph)\n\nWe have a suspicion, but no answer: the keyword points to the cruciate ligament, the similar texts point to a sprain. Similar is not the same. The knee knowledge graph knows anatomy, findings, injury mechanisms and injury patterns – as rules a machine can check and explain.\n\n**Question:** Do Jonas' findings fit the pattern of a sprain or of a cruciate ligament rupture – and why?\n\n- **Features:** RDF Graph: `SEM_MATCH` (SPARQL in SQL), rulebase and entailment, graph collection with the Foundational Model of Anatomy (FMA), join of RDF and relational data\n- **Pattern:** M1 – find, then check (part 2: check)\n- **Data:** RDF network `RDF_NETWORK` of the user DOAG2026: graph `KNEE_KG` (knee knowledge graph), graph `FMA`, entailment `KNEE_KG_RIX` (OWLPRIME + rulebase `KNEE_RB`), graph collection `KNEE_FMA_VM`\n- **Run:** as the demo user (DOAG2026) in SQL Developer for VS Code, after `08_create_rdf_network.sql`, `09a_load_knee_kg.sql` and `09b_load_fma.sql`. Run the cells from top to bottom.\n\n`SEM_MATCH` needs Oracle Java in the Autonomous AI Database. Network owner (`DOAG2026`) and network name (`RDF_NETWORK`) are the last two arguments of every call; adjust them if your demo user has another name. Prefix `kg:` = `https://doag2026.example/knee#`; Jonas' report is the resource `kg:Report_SR-2026-11-16-0001`.\n\n> Fictitious demo: no medical device and no medical advice. Places from OpenStreetMap (© OpenStreetMap contributors, ODbL); all persons and story places are synthetic."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 1
    value: "### Session settings\n\nThe story takes place on Monday, 16 November 2026, in Franconia. With the session time zone `Europe/Berlin`, all timestamps and literals read in local time.\n\nThe SQLcl settings in the second cell make the text output readable: columns as wide as their content instead of their declared length (`SQLFORMAT ansiconsole`; values from JSON are `VARCHAR2(4000)`), one header per result (`PAGESIZE`), wider lines (`LINESIZE`) and complete JSON documents and CLOBs (`LONG`, `LONGCHUNKSIZE`)."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "ALTER SESSION SET TIME_ZONE = 'Europe/Berlin';"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "SET SQLFORMAT ansiconsole\nSET PAGESIZE 1000\nSET LINESIZE 250\nSET LONG 1000000\nSET LONGCHUNKSIZE 250"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 1. What is in the graphs?\n\nThe knowledge comes from two graphs: our own knee graph and the Foundational Model of Anatomy, the reference ontology of human anatomy.\n\n**Question:** What do the two graphs contain?\n\n**How the query answers it:** Each RDF graph has a view `RDF_NETWORK#RDFT_<GRAPH>` with one row per triple (subject, predicate, object).\n\n- `KNEE_KG`: anatomy of the knee, findings, injury mechanisms, injury patterns as rules, synonyms – and Jonas' report as resource.\n- `FMA`: the Foundational Model of Anatomy, the reference ontology for human anatomy (CC BY 3.0).\n\n**Result:** 353 triples in `KNEE_KG` and 612,982 in `FMA` – the complete reference ontology, loaded as it is published. The knee graph is small and written for this case; through `skos:exactMatch` its 13 anatomical classes point into the large FMA."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT 'KNEE_KG' AS rdf_graph, COUNT(*) AS triples FROM rdf_network#rdft_knee_kg\nUNION ALL\nSELECT 'FMA',                  COUNT(*)            FROM rdf_network#rdft_fma;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 2. Jonas' report in the knowledge graph\n\nJonas' app report is also in the graph, with the findings and the mechanism taken from his answers.\n\n**Question:** Which findings and which mechanism does the graph know for Jonas?\n\n**How the query answers it:** The app report is also a resource in the graph: findings (`kg:reportsFinding`), mechanism (`kg:reportsMechanism`), affected structure, side and patient. `SEM_MATCH` runs a SPARQL query and returns the variables as columns; `OPTIONAL` adds the German label where there is one.\n\n**Result:** Jonas' report as data: a twist (*Verdrehtrauma*) of the right knee joint with four findings – audible pop, instability, pain when walking and rapid swelling (under 2 hours; the knee swelled within about 10 minutes). Report ID and patient link it to the JSON document from 11a. What the searches in 11c had to guess from words is stated here explicitly: *Plopp* is `kg:AudiblePop`, *wackelig* is `kg:GivingWay`."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT predicate, obj AS object_value, label\nFROM   TABLE(SEM_MATCH(\n         'PREFIX kg:   <https://doag2026.example/knee#>\n          PREFIX rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>\n          PREFIX skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#>\n          SELECT ?predicate ?obj ?label\n          WHERE {\n            <https://doag2026.example/knee#Report_SR-2026-11-16-0001> ?predicate ?obj .\n            OPTIONAL { ?obj rdfs:label ?label . FILTER (lang(?label) = \"de\") }\n          }',\n         SEM_MODELS('KNEE_KG'), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, ' ', NULL, NULL,\n         'DOAG2026', 'RDF_NETWORK'))\nORDER  BY predicate, obj;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 3. Check the injury patterns\n\nEach injury pattern says which findings it needs, how many of them, and which findings argue against it.\n\n**Question:** Which pattern do Jonas' findings meet – a sprain or a cruciate ligament rupture?\n\n**How the query answers it:** Each injury pattern names its strong indicators (`kg:hasStrongIndicator`), the number it needs (`kg:minStrongIndicators`) and findings that argue against it (`kg:argumentAgainst`). The SPARQL query counts per pattern how many of Jonas' findings are strong indicators and how many argue against it; SQL then decides.\n\n**Result:**\n- *Muster vordere Kreuzbandruptur* (ACL rupture): 3 of 3 strong indicators – Plopp, rapid swelling, giving way – 2 needed, nothing against it. **Fits.**\n- *Muster Kniedistorsion* (sprain): only one of the two indicators it needs (pain when walking), and three findings argue against it. **Does not fit.**\n- The other three patterns find none of their strong indicators in Jonas' report. Meniscus lesion would at least match the twist, but has no indicator; the contusion has a finding against it.\n\nThe vote in 11c said sprain. The rules say: no pattern has more findings against it than the sprain – three.\n\n*\"Similar is not the same. Not every swollen knee is a sprain.\"*"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT pattern_label,\n       TO_NUMBER(strong_found)   AS strong_found,\n       TO_NUMBER(strong_needed)  AS strong_needed,\n       TO_NUMBER(counter_found)  AS counter_found,\n       TO_NUMBER(mechanism_ok)   AS mechanism_matches,\n       CASE WHEN TO_NUMBER(strong_found) >= TO_NUMBER(strong_needed)\n             AND TO_NUMBER(counter_found) = 0\n             AND TO_NUMBER(mechanism_ok)  > 0\n            THEN 'fits' ELSE 'does not fit' END AS verdict\nFROM   TABLE(SEM_MATCH(\n         'PREFIX kg:   <https://doag2026.example/knee#>\n          PREFIX rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>\n          PREFIX skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#>\n          SELECT ?pattern_label ?strong_needed\n                 (COUNT(DISTINCT ?strong)  AS ?strong_found)\n                 (COUNT(DISTINCT ?counter) AS ?counter_found)\n                 (COUNT(DISTINCT ?mech)    AS ?mechanism_ok)\n          WHERE {\n            ?pattern a kg:InjuryPattern ;\n                     kg:minStrongIndicators ?strong_needed ;\n                     rdfs:label ?pattern_label .\n            FILTER (lang(?pattern_label) = \"de\")\n            OPTIONAL { <https://doag2026.example/knee#Report_SR-2026-11-16-0001> kg:reportsFinding ?strong .\n                       ?pattern kg:hasStrongIndicator ?strong }\n            OPTIONAL { <https://doag2026.example/knee#Report_SR-2026-11-16-0001> kg:reportsFinding ?counter .\n                       ?pattern kg:argumentAgainst ?counter }\n            OPTIONAL { <https://doag2026.example/knee#Report_SR-2026-11-16-0001> kg:reportsMechanism ?mech .\n                       ?pattern kg:requiresMechanism ?mech }\n          }\n          GROUP BY ?pattern_label ?strong_needed',\n         SEM_MODELS('KNEE_KG'), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, ' ', NULL, NULL,\n         'DOAG2026', 'RDF_NETWORK'))\nORDER  BY TO_NUMBER(strong_found) DESC, TO_NUMBER(counter_found), pattern_label;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 4. The same knowledge as rules: inferred triples\n\nThe same check can be written once as rules and computed in advance by the reasoner.\n\n**Question:** What does the reasoner derive from Jonas' report?\n\n**How the query answers it:** The rulebase `KNEE_RB` holds three rules (see `08_create_rdf_network.sql`): a reported finding that is a strong indicator of a pattern creates `kg:showsIndicatorOf`, a counter-indicator creates `kg:showsCounterIndicatorOf`, a matching mechanism `kg:matchesMechanismOf`. The entailment `KNEE_KG_RIX` has derived these triples in advance. Passing `SEM_RULEBASES('OWLPRIME', 'KNEE_RB')` makes them visible to the query.\n\nAn RDF graph stores each triple only once: three strong indicators of the same pattern derive the same triple *report – `kg:showsIndicatorOf` – pattern*. The rules therefore say *whether* a relation holds, not how often; section 3 counts. The query shows one row per pattern with the derived relations.\n\n**Result:** the rules confirm section 3 without a single `COUNT` in SPARQL. The cruciate ligament rupture is the only pattern with an indicator, the matching mechanism and no counter-indicator. The sprain has all three relations – including the counter-indicator. The contusion only has a counter-indicator, the meniscus lesion only the mechanism. The medial ligament strain is missing: Jonas' report has no relation to it at all."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT pattern_label,\n       MAX(CASE WHEN relation = 'https://doag2026.example/knee#showsIndicatorOf'        THEN 'yes' END) AS shows_indicator,\n       MAX(CASE WHEN relation = 'https://doag2026.example/knee#showsCounterIndicatorOf' THEN 'yes' END) AS shows_counter_indicator,\n       MAX(CASE WHEN relation = 'https://doag2026.example/knee#matchesMechanismOf'      THEN 'yes' END) AS matches_mechanism\nFROM   TABLE(SEM_MATCH(\n         'PREFIX kg:   <https://doag2026.example/knee#>\n          PREFIX rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>\n          PREFIX skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#>\n          SELECT ?relation ?pattern_label\n          WHERE {\n            <https://doag2026.example/knee#Report_SR-2026-11-16-0001> ?relation ?pattern .\n            ?pattern rdfs:label ?pattern_label .\n            FILTER (?relation IN (kg:showsIndicatorOf, kg:showsCounterIndicatorOf, kg:matchesMechanismOf))\n            FILTER (lang(?pattern_label) = \"de\")\n          }',\n         SEM_MODELS('KNEE_KG'), SEM_RULEBASES('OWLPRIME', 'KNEE_RB'), NULL, NULL, NULL, ' ', NULL, NULL,\n         'DOAG2026', 'RDF_NETWORK'))\nGROUP  BY pattern_label\nORDER  BY pattern_label;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 5. The explanation: from the pattern to the structure in the FMA\n\nA diagnosis without a reason is not trustworthy. The graph can follow the chain from the pattern to the anatomy and the next step.\n\n**Question:** Which structure is injured, what is it called in the reference anatomy, and what should happen next?\n\n**How the query answers it:** The graph collection `KNEE_FMA_VM` combines `KNEE_KG`, the `FMA` and the inferred triples, so one query can follow the chain:\n\npattern → suggests injury (`kg:suggests`) → affects structure (`kg:affects`) → same as FMA class (`skos:exactMatch`) → FMA label, plus recommendation (`kg:recommends`) and urgency (`kg:urgencyHours`).\n\n**Result:** ACL rupture → *anterior cruciate ligament* = FMA 44614 (*Anterior cruciate ligament*) → MRI within 48 hours."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT injury,\n       structure,\n       REGEXP_SUBSTR(fma_class, 'FMA_[0-9]+')  AS fma_id,\n       fma_label,\n       recommendation,\n       TO_NUMBER(urgency_hours)                AS urgency_hours\nFROM   TABLE(SEM_MATCH(\n         'PREFIX kg:   <https://doag2026.example/knee#>\n          PREFIX rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>\n          PREFIX skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#>\n          SELECT ?injury ?structure ?fma_class ?fma_label ?recommendation ?urgency_hours\n          WHERE {\n            kg:Pattern_ACL kg:suggests     ?i ;\n                           kg:recommends   ?p ;\n                           kg:urgencyHours ?urgency_hours .\n            ?i rdfs:label ?injury .          FILTER (lang(?injury) = \"en\")\n            ?i kg:affects ?s .\n            ?s rdfs:label ?structure .       FILTER (lang(?structure) = \"en\")\n            ?p rdfs:label ?recommendation .  FILTER (lang(?recommendation) = \"en\")\n            OPTIONAL { ?s skos:exactMatch ?fma_class .\n                       OPTIONAL { ?fma_class rdfs:label ?fma_label } }\n          }',\n         SEM_MODELS('KNEE_FMA_VM'), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, ' ', NULL, NULL,\n         'DOAG2026', 'RDF_NETWORK'));"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 6. The graph knows the synonyms\n\nIn step 3 someone had to type the synonyms of \"Plopp\" by hand.\n\n**Question:** Which words for an audible pop does the graph know?\n\n**How the query answers it:** In step 3 someone had to type `Knall OR geknallt OR Schnappen` by hand. The knowledge graph stores these words as `skos:altLabel` of the finding *AudiblePop*. `LISTAGG` turns them into a ready-made Oracle Text query; braces `{…}` protect multi-word phrases.\n\n**Result:** `{Knall} OR {Plopp} OR {Schnappen} OR {geknallt}` – exactly the three synonyms typed by hand in 11c, plus *Plopp* itself. Nobody has to know them any more; they are maintained once, in the graph, and every search can use them."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT LISTAGG('{' || alt_label || '}', ' OR ') WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY alt_label) AS contains_query\nFROM   TABLE(SEM_MATCH(\n         'PREFIX kg:   <https://doag2026.example/knee#>\n          PREFIX rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>\n          PREFIX skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#>\n          SELECT ?alt_label\n          WHERE { kg:AudiblePop skos:altLabel ?alt_label }',\n         SEM_MODELS('KNEE_KG'), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, ' ', NULL, NULL,\n         'DOAG2026', 'RDF_NETWORK'));"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "### Use the synonyms from the graph in the full-text search\n\n**Question:** How many reports does the search find with the graph's vocabulary?\n\n**How the query answers it:** The PL/SQL block reads the synonyms from the graph and passes the generated query as a bind variable to `CONTAINS`. It counts the FHIR reports and the cruciate ligament ruptures among them. The output appears in the DBMS output of the cell.\n\n**Result:** 34 hits, 27 of them cruciate ligament ruptures. In 11c, \"Plopp\" alone found 12 reports with 7 ruptures; the synonyms typed by hand found 22 with 20. The query from the graph finds both sets – almost four times as many ruptures as the word Jonas used. The 7 other hits are mostly the negations again (*\"Kein Plopp\"*): the graph knows the words, but full text still cannot read *kein*. That is why step 4 judges Jonas' findings with the rules (section 3), not with a word search."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "DECLARE\n  l_query  VARCHAR2(4000);\n  l_hits   PLS_INTEGER;\n  l_acl    PLS_INTEGER;\nBEGIN\n  SELECT LISTAGG('{' || alt_label || '}', ' OR ') WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY alt_label)\n  INTO   l_query\n  FROM   TABLE(SEM_MATCH(\n         'PREFIX kg:   <https://doag2026.example/knee#>\n          PREFIX rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>\n          PREFIX skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#>\n          SELECT ?alt_label\n          WHERE { kg:AudiblePop skos:altLabel ?alt_label }',\n         SEM_MODELS('KNEE_KG'), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, ' ', NULL, NULL,\n         'DOAG2026', 'RDF_NETWORK'));\n\n  SELECT COUNT(*),\n         COUNT(CASE WHEN d.diagnosis_code = 'vkb_ruptur' THEN 1 END)\n  INTO   l_hits, l_acl\n  FROM   symptom_reports s\n  LEFT   JOIN diagnoses d ON d.report_id = s.report_id\n  WHERE  s.source = 'fhir'\n  AND    CONTAINS(s.report_text, l_query) > 0;\n\n  DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('Query from the graph: ' || l_query);\n  DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('Hits: ' || l_hits || ', of them cruciate ligament ruptures: ' || l_acl);\nEND;\n/"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 7. Knowledge meets case data: RDF and relational in one query\n\nKnowledge is only useful if it connects to real cases.\n\n**Question:** How often were the injuries the graph suggests actually diagnosed – and confirmed by MRI?\n\n**How the query answers it:** The injuries in the graph carry the diagnosis code of the case data (`kg:caseDiagnosisCode`, e.g. `vkb_ruptur`). So the result of `SEM_MATCH` can be joined with the relational table `DIAGNOSES` like any other row source.\n\n**Result:** the sprain is the most frequent knee diagnosis in the 1,000 records (115), ahead of the cruciate ligament rupture (80) and medial ligament strain and meniscus lesion (61 each). That frequency is why the nearest neighbours in 11c voted for the sprain. But all 80 ruptures and all 61 meniscus lesions were confirmed by MRI, none of the sprains: a sprain is a clinical diagnosis – a diagnosis of exclusion, as the rules in section 3 say. Frequent is not the same as likely for Jonas."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT k.pattern_label,\n       k.injury_label,\n       k.diagnosis_code,\n       COUNT(d.diagnosis_id)                                    AS diagnoses,\n       COUNT(CASE WHEN d.confirmed_by = 'MRT' THEN 1 END)       AS confirmed_by_mri\nFROM   TABLE(SEM_MATCH(\n         'PREFIX kg:   <https://doag2026.example/knee#>\n          PREFIX rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>\n          PREFIX skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#>\n          SELECT ?pattern_label ?injury_label ?diagnosis_code\n          WHERE {\n            ?pattern a kg:InjuryPattern ;\n                     rdfs:label  ?pattern_label ;\n                     kg:suggests ?injury .\n            ?injury rdfs:label ?injury_label ;\n                    kg:caseDiagnosisCode ?diagnosis_code .\n            FILTER (lang(?pattern_label) = \"de\")\n            FILTER (lang(?injury_label)  = \"en\")\n          }',\n         SEM_MODELS('KNEE_KG'), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, ' ', NULL, NULL,\n         'DOAG2026', 'RDF_NETWORK')) k\nLEFT   JOIN diagnoses d ON d.diagnosis_code = k.diagnosis_code\nGROUP  BY k.pattern_label, k.injury_label, k.diagnosis_code\nORDER  BY diagnoses DESC, k.pattern_label;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "### Summary\n\n- The vector search found similar cases, the knowledge graph checks them against rules: Jonas' findings fit the **ACL rupture** pattern (3 of 3 strong indicators), not the sprain pattern (3 counter-indicators).\n- The answer comes with its reason: findings → pattern → anterior cruciate ligament (FMA 44614) → **MRI within 48 hours**.\n- Next: **11f** – who can do the MRI in time, and where?"
    languageId: markdown
